# .env.example — claire-dashboard
#
# Copy to `.env` if you ever need to override a value locally:
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# HONEST SCOPE — READ THIS FIRST
# claire-dashboard is a *static* Vite + React single-page app. It has no
# backend, no database, no cache or queue, and it reads NO runtime secrets.
# The datasets it renders are committed CSVs under public/data/ that the
# browser fetches at runtime (see src/data/loader.ts) — there is no API key,
# connection string or token to configure. Do not go looking for phantom keys:
# if a variable is not listed below, the project does not read it.
#
# The one value the code reads is Vite's built-in BASE_URL, and it is a
# BUILD-TIME setting, not a runtime secret. It is documented here so the build
# is reproducible and the next person understands the only knob that exists.


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# BASE_URL — the public base path the app is served under.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT IT IS
#   Read in code as `import.meta.env.BASE_URL` (src/data/loader.ts), where it
#   is used to resolve the CSV paths — e.g. `${BASE_URL}data/sales_weekly.csv`
#   — so the app works whether it is served from the domain root or a
#   sub-path.
#
# WHERE IT IS SET (this is NOT set via a .env var)
#   BASE_URL is derived by Vite from the `base` option, NOT from this file.
#   - Config:      vite.config.ts -> `base: "./"`  (current value: relative,
#                  so the app runs correctly from any sub-path or file:// —
#                  this is why `docker compose up` and the static preview both
#                  work without extra config).
#   - Build flag:  override per build with `vite build --base=/my/path/`.
#   Change `base` in vite.config.ts (or pass --base) to relocate the app;
#   there is no environment variable named BASE_URL to set here.
#
# PLACEHOLDER (informational — shows the shape of the value, not a var to set)
#   BASE_URL=/
